# Verique (TrustLens) — How the Project Works

Verique is an **AI-powered fact-checking system** that extracts claims from text, searches the web for evidence, and produces verdicts with confidence scores. It has **3 components**: a Python backend, a Next.js frontend, and a Chrome extension.

---

## High-Level Architecture

```mermaid
graph LR
    subgraph "User Interfaces"
        A["Next.js Frontend\n(localhost:3000)"]
        B["Chrome Extension"]
    end

    subgraph "Backend (FastAPI)"
        C["API Layer\n/api/v1/verify"]
        D["VerificationService"]
        E["LangGraph Pipeline\n(8 Agents)"]
    end

    subgraph "External Services"
        F["Groq API\n(Llama 3 LLM)"]
        G["DuckDuckGo / SerpAPI\n/ Google CSE"]
    end

    subgraph "Data Layer"
        H["SQLite / PostgreSQL"]
        I["In-Memory Cache"]
    end

    A -- "POST /api/v1/verify/" --> C
    B -- "POST /api/v1/verify" --> C
    C --> D --> E
    E --> F
    E --> G
    D --> H
    D --> I
```

---

## 1. Backend (`backend/`)

**Stack:** FastAPI + SQLAlchemy + LangGraph + Groq SDK

### Entry Point — [main.py](file:///C:/Users/Saad/OneDrive/Desktop/Projects/Verique/backend/app/main.py)

- Creates the FastAPI app with CORS, structured logging, and a lifespan manager
- Mounts all API routes under the prefix set in config (defaults to `/v1`, but the app itself is also prefixed with `/api` via the router)
- On startup, initializes the database tables

### Configuration — [config.py](file:///C:/Users/Saad/OneDrive/Desktop/Projects/Verique/backend/app/core/config.py)

Loads settings from [.env](file:///C:/Users/Saad/OneDrive/Desktop/Projects/Verique/backend/.env):
- **LLM**: Uses Groq API (free) with `llama-3.3-70b-versatile` and `llama-3.1-8b-instant`
- **Search**: DuckDuckGo (free, default), with optional SerpAPI / Google CSE
- **Database**: PostgreSQL in production, SQLite for local dev
- **Rate limiting**: 10 req/min, 100 req/hour

### Database — [database.py](file:///C:/Users/Saad/OneDrive/Desktop/Projects/Verique/backend/app/core/database.py)

- Auto-detects SQLite vs PostgreSQL from `DATABASE_URL`
- Uses async SQLAlchemy (`aiosqlite` or `asyncpg`)
- Creates tables on startup: `articles`, [claims](file:///C:/Users/Saad/OneDrive/Desktop/Projects/Verique/backend/app/agents/verification.py#108-158), `claim_verdicts`, `verifications`, `sources`, `verification_sources`, `domains`

### API Layer — [verification.py (endpoint)](file:///C:/Users/Saad/OneDrive/Desktop/Projects/Verique/backend/app/api/v1/endpoints/verification.py)

| Endpoint | Method | Description |
|---|---|---|
| `/api/v1/verify/` | POST | Verify raw text content |
| `/api/v1/verify/url` | POST | Fetch a URL and verify its content |
| `/api/v1/verify/{id}` | GET | Get verification status (not yet implemented) |
| `/api/v1/verify/async` | POST | Start async verification job (stub) |

When a POST hits `/api/v1/verify/`:
1. Checks cache for a previous result with the same content hash
2. If not cached → runs the [VerificationService](file:///C:/Users/Saad/OneDrive/Desktop/Projects/Verique/backend/app/services/verification_service.py#15-197) pipeline
3. Caches the result and returns it

---

### The Multi-Agent Pipeline — [pipeline.py](file:///C:/Users/Saad/OneDrive/Desktop/Projects/Verique/backend/app/agents/pipeline.py)

This is the **core of the system**. It uses **LangGraph** to orchestrate 8 agents in a linear state machine:

```mermaid
graph TD
    A["1. Ingestion Agent"] --> B["2. Claim Decomposer"]
    B --> C["3. Claim Classifier"]
    C --> D["4. Query Planner"]
    D --> E["5. Retrieval Agent"]
    E --> F["6. Evidence Ranker"]
    F --> G["7. Verification Agent"]
    G --> H["8. Explanation Agent"]
```

Each agent receives a shared [PipelineState](file:///C:/Users/Saad/OneDrive/Desktop/Projects/Verique/backend/app/agents/pipeline.py#36-73) dict and adds its output:

| # | Agent | File | What it does |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | **IngestionAgent** | [ingestion.py](file:///C:/Users/Saad/OneDrive/Desktop/Projects/Verique/backend/app/agents/ingestion.py) | Cleans raw text (strips HTML, normalizes whitespace) |
| 2 | **ClaimDecomposerAgent** | [claim_decomposer.py](file:///C:/Users/Saad/OneDrive/Desktop/Projects/Verique/backend/app/agents/claim_decomposer.py) | Calls Groq LLM to extract factual claims (numeric, entity, temporal, comparative, causal) |
| 3 | **ClaimClassifierAgent** | [claim_classifier.py](file:///C:/Users/Saad/OneDrive/Desktop/Projects/Verique/backend/app/agents/claim_classifier.py) | Filters out non-verifiable claims (opinions, hypotheticals) |
| 4 | **QueryPlannerAgent** | [query_planner.py](file:///C:/Users/Saad/OneDrive/Desktop/Projects/Verique/backend/app/agents/query_planner.py) | Generates targeted search queries for each claim |
| 5 | **RetrievalAgent** | [retrieval.py](file:///C:/Users/Saad/OneDrive/Desktop/Projects/Verique/backend/app/agents/retrieval.py) | Searches the web (DuckDuckGo/SerpAPI/Google) in parallel, attaches domain reputation scores |
| 6 | **EvidenceRankerAgent** | [evidence_ranker.py](file:///C:/Users/Saad/OneDrive/Desktop/Projects/Verique/backend/app/agents/evidence_ranker.py) | Scores and ranks evidence by relevance and domain quality |
| 7 | **VerificationAgent** | [verification.py](file:///C:/Users/Saad/OneDrive/Desktop/Projects/Verique/backend/app/agents/verification.py) | Calls Groq LLM to generate a verdict for each claim based on evidence |
| 8 | **ExplanationAgent** | [explanation.py](file:///C:/Users/Saad/OneDrive/Desktop/Projects/Verique/backend/app/agents/explanation.py) | Formats results into the final user-friendly JSON response |

### Verdict Categories

Each claim gets one of 7 verdicts:

| Verdict | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `strongly_supported` | Multiple high-quality sources confirm |
| `supported` | Evidence generally supports |
| `mixed` | Conflicting evidence found |
| `weak` | Limited or low-quality evidence |
| `contradicted` | Evidence contradicts the claim |
| `outdated` | Claim may have been true but is no longer current |
| `not_verifiable` | Cannot find evidence either way |

### Page Score Algorithm — [verification_service.py](file:///C:/Users/Saad/OneDrive/Desktop/Projects/Verique/backend/app/services/verification_service.py)

After all claims are verified, a **page score (0–100)** is calculated using:
- Weighted average of verdict scores per claim
- Position weighting (earlier claims matter more)
- Source count bonus (logarithmic scaling)
- Support ratio bonus (sigmoid-like curve)
- Global bonus if overall support ratio is high

